<div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">

    <h2>Student Rights, Discipline, Manifestation Determinations & Due Process in District 9</h2>

    <p>In NYC District 9, encompassing the Grand Concourse, Highbridge, Morrisania, and Tremont neighborhoods, ensuring student rights and upholding fair disciplinary practices is of utmost importance. Students are entitled to a safe, supportive, and inclusive learning environment, free from all forms of discrimination, harassment, and retaliation. This right is especially crucial for students with disabilities who require specific accommodations and supports to succeed academically and behaviorally.</p>

    <h3>Manifestation Determination Reviews (MDR): A Critical Safeguard</h3>

    <p>For students with IEPs (Individualized Education Programs) or 504 plans who face suspensions exceeding 10 consecutive school days, or a series of suspensions that constitute a pattern of exclusion (even if individually less than 10 days), a Manifestation Determination Review (MDR) becomes a mandatory process under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) (34 CFR § 300.530). This review is conducted by the IEP team and relevant school personnel. The central question of the MDR is whether the student's misconduct is a direct result of their disability, or whether it stems from the school's failure to appropriately implement the student's IEP. This includes fidelity to providing mandated services, accommodations, and behavioral supports.</p>

    <p><strong>The MDR Process Explained: A Step-by-Step Guide for Parents</strong></p>

    <ol>
        <li><strong>Notification:</strong> Upon reaching the threshold for an MDR (suspension exceeding 10 school days or a pattern of removals), the school is legally obligated to notify you, the parent, of the need for an MDR meeting. This notification should be timely and provide adequate notice (typically at least 5 school days) to allow you to prepare.</li>
        <li><strong>Record Review:</strong> Before the MDR meeting, carefully review your child’s IEP, any behavioral intervention plans (BIPs), school discipline records, attendance records, and any relevant evaluations or reports from outside professionals (e.g., therapists, psychologists). Document any instances where the IEP wasn't followed or where recommended supports weren't provided.</li>
        <li><strong>MDR Meeting:</strong> Attend the MDR meeting prepared to actively participate. The IEP team will review the student's file and consider the following:
            <ul>
                <li>Was the conduct in question caused by or directly and substantially related to the child's disability? (e.g., a student with Tourette’s Syndrome making involuntary vocalizations that disrupt the classroom).</li>
                <li>Was the conduct a direct result of the school's failure to implement the IEP? (e.g., a student with ADHD being suspended for inattention when the IEP requires preferential seating near the teacher, which was never provided).</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Presenting Your Case:</strong> As a parent, you have the right to present information and perspectives at the MDR meeting. Bring any evidence that supports your claim that the behavior is related to your child's disability or the school's failure to implement the IEP. This could include:
            <ul>
                <li>Letters from therapists or doctors</li>
                <li>Personal observations of your child's behavior</li>
                <li>Documentation of IEP non-compliance</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>MDR Determination:</strong> The IEP team must reach a consensus decision. If the team determines that the behavior *is* a manifestation of the disability or *is* due to IEP implementation failure, the following must occur:
            <ul>
                <li>The IEP team must conduct a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA), unless one has already been conducted, *and* implement a Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP) for the student. If a BIP is already in place, the IEP team *must* review and modify it to address the behavior. (34 CFR § 300.530(f)(1)).</li>
                <li>The student must be returned to their placement *unless* the parent and the school agree to a change of placement as part of the BIP development or modification process.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
         <li><strong>If the Behavior is *Not* a Manifestation:</strong> If the team determines that the behavior is *not* a manifestation of the disability, the student can be disciplined in the same manner as students without disabilities. *However*, the school still has an obligation to provide services during the period of suspension to ensure the student continues to participate in the general education curriculum and progresses toward meeting their IEP goals. (34 CFR § 300.530(d)).</li>
         <li><strong>Parental Disagreement:</strong> If you disagree with the MDR determination, you have the right to challenge the decision through impartial due process hearing (more on this below).</li>
    </ol>

     <p><strong>District 9 Specific Considerations for MDRs:</strong></p>

     <ul>
        <li><strong>High-Needs Student Population:</strong> District 9 serves a diverse student population with a high percentage of students with disabilities. It's crucial that the IEP teams are adequately trained and resourced to conduct thorough and individualized MDRs. Parents should advocate for comprehensive assessments and evidence-based interventions.</li>
        <li><strong>Language Access:</strong> Ensure that all communication and documentation related to the MDR are provided in your native language. District 9 has a significant Spanish-speaking population, so language access is critical.</li>
        <li><strong>Community Resources:</strong> Connect with local advocacy organizations in the Bronx that can provide support and guidance during the MDR process. (See contact information at the end of this page).</li>
     </ul>

    <h3>Navigating Suspensions in District 9: A Parent's Guide</h3>

    <p>If your child is facing suspension, it's essential to understand your rights and take proactive steps to protect your child's educational interests.</p>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>Know Your Rights:</strong> Familiarize yourself with the NYC Department of Education's discipline code, known as the "Citywide Behavioral Expectations to Support Student Learning." This document outlines the types of infractions that can lead to suspension and the procedures that must be followed. Pay close attention to the sections related to students with disabilities. Also, be aware of your rights under IDEA.</li>
        <li><strong>Immediate Actions:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Request Written Notification:</strong> Immediately request a written explanation of the reasons for the suspension, the duration of the suspension, and the specific charges against your child.</li>
                <li><strong>Review School Records:</strong> Request access to your child's school records, including incident reports, witness statements, and any other documentation related to the suspension.</li>
                <li><strong>Consult an Attorney or Advocate:</strong> Contact a special education attorney or parent advocate as soon as possible. They can provide legal advice, help you understand your rights, and represent you in disciplinary hearings.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Documentation is Key:</strong> Maintain detailed records of all communications with the school, including dates, times, names of individuals you spoke with, and the content of the conversations. Save all emails, letters, and other documents related to the suspension.</li>
        <li><strong>Actively Participate in Hearings:</strong> Attend all disciplinary hearings and actively participate in the process. You have the right to present evidence, question witnesses, and make arguments on your child's behalf. Prepare your child to speak honestly and respectfully.</li>
        <li><strong>Challenging the Suspension:</strong> If you believe the suspension is unwarranted or violates your child's rights, you have the right to appeal the decision. The appeal process varies depending on the length of the suspension. Consult with an attorney or advocate to determine the best course of action.</li>
        <li><strong>Alternative Dispute Resolution:</strong> Consider exploring alternative dispute resolution options, such as mediation, to resolve the conflict with the school. Mediation can be a less adversarial and more collaborative way to reach a mutually agreeable solution.</li>
        <li><strong>Request Support Services:</strong> Regardless of the outcome of the suspension, request that the school provide support services to help your child address the underlying issues that led to the suspension. This may include counseling, tutoring, or behavior intervention.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Due Process Rights: Your Legal Recourse</h3>

    <p>If you disagree with the school's decisions regarding your child's special education services, including the MDR determination, IEP development, placement, or disciplinary actions, you have the right to pursue an impartial due process hearing under IDEA (34 CFR § 300.507). This is a formal legal proceeding where you can present evidence, call witnesses, and challenge the school's actions before an impartial hearing officer.</p>

    <p><strong>Initiating a Due Process Hearing: A Step-by-Step Guide</strong></p>

    <ol>
        <li><strong>File a Due Process Complaint:</strong> The first step is to file a written due process complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED). The complaint must contain specific information, including:
            <ul>
                <li>The name and address of the child and the school</li>
                <li>A description of the problem or issue you are disputing</li>
                <li>A proposed resolution to the problem</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>NYSED Review:</strong> NYSED will review your complaint to ensure it meets the requirements of IDEA. The school district will then have an opportunity to respond to your complaint.</li>
        <li><strong>Resolution Session:</strong> Within 30 days of receiving your due process complaint, the school district must convene a resolution session with you and relevant members of the IEP team. The purpose of the resolution session is to attempt to resolve the dispute informally. You can waive this session if you choose.</li>
        <li><strong>Due Process Hearing:</strong> If the dispute is not resolved during the resolution session, a due process hearing will be scheduled. The hearing will be conducted by an impartial hearing officer (IHO) appointed by NYSED.</li>
        <li><strong>Hearing Preparation:</strong> Prepare for the hearing by gathering evidence, identifying witnesses, and developing a legal strategy. An attorney or advocate can provide invaluable assistance in this process.</li>
        <li><strong>The Hearing:</strong> At the hearing, you will have the opportunity to present your case, call witnesses, and cross-examine the school district's witnesses. The IHO will consider all the evidence and issue a written decision.</li>
        <li><strong>Appeals:</strong> If you disagree with the IHO's decision, you have the right to appeal to the State Review Officer (SRO). The SRO will review the IHO's decision and issue a final order.</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>Key Considerations for Due Process in District 9:</strong></p>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>Timelines:</strong> Due process hearings are subject to strict timelines. It's crucial to act quickly and meet all deadlines. Failure to do so could jeopardize your case.</li>
        <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> Gather as much evidence as possible to support your claims. This may include school records, evaluations, reports, and witness testimony.</li>
        <li><strong>Legal Representation:</strong> Consider retaining an experienced special education attorney to represent you in the due process hearing. An attorney can navigate the complex legal procedures and advocate effectively on your child's behalf.</li>
        <li><strong>Stay Put:</strong> During the pendency of a due process hearing, your child has the right to remain in their current educational placement (known as "stay put"). This means that the school cannot change your child's placement without your consent or a court order. (34 CFR § 300.518).</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Common District 9 Pitfalls & How to Avoid Them</h3>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>Inadequate IEPs:</strong> Ensure your child's IEP is comprehensive, individualized, and addresses all their needs. Advocate for specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound (SMART) goals. Don't accept generic IEPs that don't reflect your child's unique circumstances.</li>
        <li><strong>Lack of Parent Involvement:</strong> Actively participate in all IEP meetings and other school-related activities. Maintain regular communication with teachers and administrators. Your voice is crucial in ensuring your child receives the appropriate services and supports.</li>
        <li><strong>Delay in Evaluations:</strong> If you suspect your child has a disability, request an evaluation from the school district as soon as possible. Don't wait for the school to initiate the process. Document your requests in writing. Under NYS Part 200 regulations, the district has a specific timeframe to complete the evaluation and hold an IEP meeting.</li>
        <li><strong>Behavioral Issues Not Addressed:</strong> If your child is struggling with behavioral issues, ensure the IEP includes a comprehensive behavior intervention plan (BIP) based on a functional behavioral assessment (FBA). The BIP should outline specific strategies to address the behavior and promote positive behavior changes.</li>
        <li><strong>Lack of Accommodations:</strong> Make sure your child receives all the necessary accommodations and modifications outlined in their IEP. This may include preferential seating, extended time on tests, assistive technology, or other supports. Document any instances where accommodations are not provided.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h3>

    <dl>
        <dt><strong>Q: What is the difference between a suspension and an expulsion?</strong></dt>
        <dd>A suspension is a temporary removal from school, while an expulsion is a permanent removal. Expulsions are rare in NYC public schools, but can occur in cases of serious misconduct. Consult with an attorney immediately if your child is facing expulsion.</dd>
        <dt><strong>Q: What are my rights if my child is bullied at school?</strong></dt>
        <dd>All students have the right to a safe and supportive learning environment, free from bullying and harassment. Schools are required to have policies in place to address bullying. If your child is being bullied, report the incidents to the school administration immediately. If the school fails to take appropriate action, you may have legal recourse.</dd>
        <dt><strong>Q: Can the school drug test my child without my consent?</strong></dt>
        <dd>Generally, schools cannot drug test students without parental consent or a reasonable suspicion that the student is under the influence of drugs. Consult with an attorney if your child has been subjected to an illegal drug test.</dd>
        <dt><strong>Q: What if I can't afford an attorney?</strong></dt>
        <dd>Several organizations in New York City provide free or low-cost legal services to families of children with disabilities. Contact the organizations listed below for assistance. Additionally, you can contact the New York Legal Assistance Group (NYLAG) and the Legal Aid Society for potential representation.</dd>
        <dt><strong>Q: My child's school is not following their IEP. What should I do?</strong></dt>
        <dd>Document every instance of IEP non-compliance. Contact the school principal and special education director in writing to request immediate action. If the issue is not resolved, consider filing a formal complaint with the New York State Education Department or pursuing mediation or due process.</dd>
    </dl>

    <p><strong>Local Resources for District 9 Families:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>INCLUDEnyc:</strong> (212) 677-4660 - Provides information, training, and advocacy for families of children with disabilities in NYC.</li>
        <li><strong>Advocates for Children of New York (AFC):</strong> (212) 947-9779 - Offers legal representation and advocacy services to low-income families of students with disabilities.</li>
        <li><strong>The Bronx Independent Living Services (BILS):</strong> (718) 515-2800 - Provides services and advocacy for individuals with disabilities in the Bronx.</li>
    </ul>

</div>

